WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:02.220
Welcome to Math one oh two.

2
00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.060
This is Section five point two: volumes by disks and washers.

3
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.360
We'll turn a flat region into a solid, then let the picture guide our setup.

4
00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:12.590
Start with a cylinder.

5
00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:19.210
Slide a cross section along its axis: the area stays constant, so volume is area times length.

6
00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.210
But a curved solid has changing cross sections.

7
00:00:22.910 --> 00:00:24.030
Slice it into thin pieces.

8
00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:28.550
Each small volume is approximately cross-sectional area times thickness.

9
00:00:29.143 --> 00:00:31.323
Take a vertical strip touching the x-axis.

10
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:34.183
It is perpendicular to our rotation axis.

11
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:35.523
Watch it turn.

12
00:00:36.243 --> 00:00:38.943
The strip sweeps out a disk, filling the center completely.

13
00:00:39.603 --> 00:00:41.963
Its radius reaches from the axis to the curve.

14
00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:44.863
So the cross-sectional area is pi R squared.

15
00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:47.447
Now lift the strip away from the axis.

16
00:00:48.207 --> 00:00:49.947
Rotate it, and the gap makes a hole.

17
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:51.527
That gives a washer.

18
00:00:52.107 --> 00:00:57.947
Measure both radii from the axis: the outer radius to the far edge, and the inner radius to the near edge.

19
00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:02.847
Subtract the two circular areas: pi times R squared minus r squared.

20
00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:08.450
Let's follow the region from the notes: square root of x above x squared, from zero to one.

21
00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.390
As the strip moves, both radii change.

22
00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.490
Watch its washer slide along the same axis.

23
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.990
The outer radius grows, but so does the hole.

24
00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.350
That's why we need both curves to find the area.

25
00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:22.833
Now divide the interval into ten pieces.

26
00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:26.233
In each piece, take the two radii at the midpoint.

27
00:01:26.793 --> 00:01:29.173
Rotate those rectangles into ten washers.

28
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:32.373
Each has a constant cross section and a small thickness.

29
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.713
Together they approximate the solid.

30
00:01:35.313 --> 00:01:37.513
Add their small volumes to estimate the total.

31
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:38.700
See the stepped edges?

32
00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:40.880
Use twenty washers, then forty.

33
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.440
The thinner pieces follow the curves more closely.

34
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.860
As their widths approach zero, the sum becomes an integral.

35
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.360
Here the area is pi times x minus x to the fourth.

36
00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.000
We'll leave that integral for class.

37
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:56.600
This cutaway only reveals the inside; the integral includes the whole solid.

38
00:01:57.043 --> 00:01:59.663
For Example One, change just the lower boundary.

39
00:02:00.223 --> 00:02:04.443
Keep square root of x above, but move the bottom curve down to the x-axis.

40
00:02:05.023 --> 00:02:05.963
Watch the hole close.

41
00:02:06.623 --> 00:02:10.703
Now every strip touches the axis, so our washers become disks.

42
00:02:10.703 --> 00:02:13.303
That one geometric change simplifies the setup.

43
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:19.790
Here is the first example: rotate the region under square root of x, from zero to one, about the x-axis.

44
00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.470
Choose a vertical strip.

45
00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:25.690
Its radius is square root of x, so its disk area is pi x.

46
00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.870
The volume is pi times the integral of x from zero to one.

47
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.990
The calculation is ready for class.
